<h1 class="page-title">Cyberpedia</h1>
<p class="standfirst">Sound lead, programming assistance &middot; 2024&ndash; &middot; Bitcrush</p>

<p>Cyberpedia is a point-and-click puzzle game by Chris Erickson (Bitcrush), dressed up as a 90s educational <span class="nowrap">CD-ROM</span>: boot it up and, through the power of the internet, it builds a museum full of exhibits. Thirty years on, the links are broken, information has gone missing and a "generative" trend is spreading through the museum, so you and your assistant, Bookworth, set about putting it right.</p>
<p>I made all of its sound effects and music in 2024, and stayed on when the game won a Screen Australia grant: in 2025 it was backed by their Emerging Gamemakers Fund. An early demo is now on itch.io, with sound effects by me and music by Max Kearney and me.</p>

{% video { youtube: "sNgPT3m4pEo", title: "Cyberpedia full playthrough", caption: "A full playthrough from the end of 2024, with all my music and sound." } %}

<h2>Sounding like 1996</h2>
<p>To get the sound of a 90s kids' game, I listened to a lot of them (Treasure MathStorm!, Kid Pix, the Bill Nye theme) and noticed the same things: warbly tape saturation and sampled 80s drums. So everything went through tape emulation, the drums came from a VHS-era sample pack, the synths are deliberately cheap, and it was all exported at 11 kHz and 8-bit, like the <span class="nowrap">CD-ROM</span> games it's imitating.</p>

<h2>Sound effects</h2>
<p>The sound effects are just as 90s: menu buttons and books on the shelf that play little musical notes, a nerdy babble for Bookworth, a printer whirr when you fix an exhibit, and much more.</p>
<p>Chris built the game with Adventure Creator, a visual scripting kit for Unity that handles most sounds (on click, on hover) without code. Where it didn't, I wrote C# scripts: the dialogue babble, the cursor sounds and the music changes between stages.</p>

